// `CONNECT` through a proxy — and the file that shows last week's finding was about the wrong half.
//
// `packages/http/src/proxy.wac` is 257 lines and nothing here had predicted anything about it. It
// opens a tunnel and stops, leaving a socket that is no longer carrying HTTP, and its header names
// three things that are easy to get wrong. One of them is the finding this file is for:
//
// > **Bytes past the blank line are kept.** A proxy may put the end of its reply and the start of the
// > tunnel in one packet … Dropping the remainder loses the first record of any protocol whose far
// > end speaks first. TLS is not one — the client sends the hello — so this is precisely the case
// > that passes everywhere it is tested and fails against something else later. `Established` carries
// > the offset for that reason.

import { Result, Vec, Bytes } from "core";
import { Net, Socket, NotGranted } from "std";

/**
 * Where a proxy is.
 *
 * `ProxyAt { bool ok; string host; i32 port; }` in the shipped file — an `ok` flag and two fields
 * that mean nothing when it is false, which `Result` replaces. What `Result` also buys here is the
 * reason, and there are **five distinct ones answering the same `ProxyAt(false, "", 0)`**: an empty
 * authority, an empty host, an empty port, a non-digit in the port, and a port of zero or above
 * 65535. An operator whose `HTTP_PROXY` is wrong gets none of them.
 */
export struct ProxyAt { string host; i32 port; }

export struct NoAuthority { string url; }
export struct NoHost { string url; }
export struct NoPort { string url; }
export struct PortNotDigits { string url; u8 saw; }
export struct PortOutOfRange { string url; i64 saw; }
export union<NoAuthority, NoHost, NoPort, PortNotDigits, PortOutOfRange> ProxyUrlFault;

/**
 * `http://gateway:3128`, the shape `HTTP_PROXY` and `HTTPS_PROXY` carry.
 *
 * The scheme is accepted and ignored, which the shipped file explains and is right about: `https://`
 * in a proxy variable means *the proxy to use for https*, not *speak TLS to the proxy*.
 */
export Result<ProxyAt, ProxyUrlFault> parseProxy(string url) { … }

/** The request that opens a tunnel. No `Connection: close` — it would ask the proxy to hang up. */
export Bytes connectRequest(string host, i32 port) { … }

/**
 * What a proxy's reply says, given everything read so far.
 *
 * **`Established(Bytes tail)`, where the shipped arm is `Established(i32 at)`** — see §1. And two
 * refusals rather than one, because a proxy's *code* decides who fixes it — see §2.
 */
export enum ProxyReply {
  /** The blank line has not arrived. Read more and ask again. */
  NeedMore,
  /** A tunnel is open. `tail` is what arrived early and belongs to the tunnel. */
  Established(Bytes tail),
  /** The proxy answered with a status. `line` is its own first line, verbatim. */
  Refused(i32 status, string line),
  /** Not an HTTP reply at all. */
  NotHttp(string line)
}

/** Read a proxy's reply out of what has arrived so far. */
export ProxyReply readReply(Bytes got) { … }

/** Open a tunnel. The only function here that owns a socket, which is the shipped file's rule. */
export async Result<Socket, ProxyFault> connectThrough(Net net, const ProxyAt at, string host, i32 port) { … }

export struct ProxyRefused { i32 status; string line; }
export struct ProxyNotHttp { string line; }
export union<ProxyRefused, ProxyNotHttp, NotGranted> ProxyFault;

// ## 1. `Established(i32 at)` is the cursor/fault finding, in a success value
//
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` counted 62 structs carrying a position, found 7 carrying the thing it
// indexes and 55 not, and framed it as **a cursor holds both halves and a fault holds one**. This arm
// is neither a cursor nor a fault and has the same defect:
//
//     Established(i32 at)     // the first byte that belongs to the tunnel
//
// `at` is an offset into the buffer the caller passed to `readReply`, so the caller must keep that
// buffer and slice it. Which is exactly what the shipped `connectThrough` does, and it works — and
// the entry's framing does not cover it, because the value is a **success**.
//
// So the rule is wider than the sweep said and simpler:
//
// > **A position is meaningless without the thing it indexes, wherever it appears.** Cursors get this
// > right because they were built to be read from; every other producer writes down the half it had
// > in a local variable, and *fault* was a coincidence of where I looked.
//
// The sweep's own numbers say so and I did not read them that way: of the 55 that carry a position
// and not its subject, **20 are not named like faults**. I reported the 35 and treated the other 20 as
// noise. They are the rest of the finding.
//
// `Established(Bytes tail)` is also the better answer for a second reason, which is that the caller
// does not want the offset. It wants the bytes. Every use of `at` in the shipped file is
// `slice(got, at, got.len())`, which is the slice the arm could have handed over.
//
// ## 2. One `Refused` for two operator actions
//
//     Refused(string line)    // "The proxy answered and said no. The text is its own first line."
//
// The header explains why the text is kept — *Squid answers `403` for a host that is not on its
// allowlist, and that is operational information rather than a network error* — and the text is all
// there is. A proxy has two refusals a caller can act on and they need opposite fixes:
//
//   * **403** — the host is not on the allowlist. Somebody edits a file on the machine running the
//     proxy.
//   * **407** — the proxy wants credentials. The *program* supplies a `Proxy-Authorization` header
//     and can retry without anyone being asked.
//
// So one of the two is recoverable in-process and the other is not, and they are one arm. `Refused(i32
// status, string line)` splits them for the cost of a field the parser already has — `readReply` must
// read the status line to know it is a refusal at all, and then keeps only the text.
//
// **That is the `Take.Accept` shape again**: the producer had the discriminating value in hand and
// dropped it because the arm it was building did not have a slot. Fourth instance, and the first where
// the dropped value is a *number the wire carried*, rather than something the producer computed.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`parseProxy` has five faults and the shipped function has one answer, and I cannot show the
// difference costs anything.** No test names a bad proxy URL, no issue reports one, and the container
// sets `HTTP_PROXY` from a file that has been right since it was written. So this is a redesign whose
// benefit is *an operator would get a sentence instead of a timeout*, argued from the shape rather
// than from an incident — which is weaker than most entries here and is worth marking as such.
//
// **`PortOutOfRange { i64 saw; }` needs an `i64` to report an `i32` field's overflow.** The port is
// parsed into something wider than it will be stored in, precisely so the fault can say what was
// written; the shipped loop checks `port > 65535` inside the accumulation and answers false, so it
// never has the whole number. A parser that wants to report the value it rejected has to be able to
// hold it, and *the type that can hold what I must reject* is a size larger than the one that holds
// what I accept — which nothing states and which is the second reason a fault's payload is not the
// value's type.
//
// **`connectThrough` answers `ProxyFault` and `parseProxy` answers `ProxyUrlFault` and they never
// meet.** A caller does both, so its own error type is `union<ProxyUrlFault, ProxyFault>` — which
// nests, and which `@/packages/box/src/gunzip.wac` shows is matchable as a group. What cannot be
// written is the *order*: `parseProxy` runs once at startup and `connectThrough` per connection, so
// the caller wants the first as a fatal and the second as a retry, and the union that joins them
// erases exactly that distinction. Provenance again — *which of my two calls failed* — and the third
// time it has appeared as **two faults in one union that a caller must tell apart by remembering
// where it was**.
